# The zero pipeline's manifest: its phases, its stages and its packages.
#
# Three kinds of line are read here, by three readers, and nothing else is:
#
#   phases P...         the phase list, read by tools/pipeline.sh as PHASE_LIST.
#                       It is here and not in tools/pipeline.sh because that file is
#                       hashed into every whim stage's key and every whim edit's: a
#                       zero phase added there would re-key all of whim.  Nothing
#                       hashes this file.
#   stage, need, apart  the schedule, read by tools/stages.sh exactly as for whim
#                       (pipes/whim.stages says what each means).  A stage is a run
#                       of split phases sharing one sweep; only a stage's end is a
#                       boundary.
#   package, uses       the concept view, read by tools/packages.sh and by nothing
#                       that runs a phase.  `uses A:P B:Q KIND why`, KIND being
#                       `mechanical` or `rationale`, as pipes/whim.stages defines.
#
# tools/stages.sh zero --check requires the stages to cover the `phases` line
# exactly once and in order; tools/packages.sh zero --check requires every phase in
# exactly one package.  `make zero-verify` and `make zero-tip` run the second.
#
# There are seven phases.  Phases are added one at a time, each on its own request
# (ZERO-GOAL.md): write the program, declare its delta in pipes/zero.delta, add it
# to `phases`, place it in a stage and a package.

phases      0 1 2 3 4 5 6

#   stage   phases
stage       0
stage       1
stage       2
stage       3
stage       4
stage       5
stage       6

# What the schedule must respect (pipes/whim.stages defines the two words).
#   apart 2 4   phase 2's check runs the binary it was handed AND the one it made
#               with `-e -s` and requires both to exit 0 -- which is how it proves
#               `--`, `+cmd` and `-T` still work.  Phase 4 removes `-e`, so that
#               check cannot run on a tree phase 4 has touched.  Every zero phase
#               is its own stage today, so this is a statement rather than a
#               constraint; it becomes one the moment two of them share a sweep.
apart       2 4
# Phase 4's check names EDIT_STDIN, read_cmd_fd = 2, had_minmin, buflist_add and
# ME_TOO_MANY_ARGS one by one and requires each to be THERE -- "it is the argv
# phase's to take" -- and phase 5 takes all five.  Measured on a phase 5 tree:
# `'EDIT_STDIN' went, and it is the argv phase's to take`, exit 1.  Phase 2's check
# would fail on a phase 5 tree too (it asserts `had_minmin = TRUE;` once, and runs
# both its binaries on `./vim f.txt`), but a stage holding 2 and 5 holds 4, so
# `apart 2 4` already forbids it; it fails on a phase 4 tree first, which is what
# that line records.
apart       4 5
# NO `apart 2 6`, AND THE MEASUREMENT IS WHY.  Phase 2's check drives a pty with
# `GA-typed<Esc>:set term?<CR>:wq<CR>` and reads the file back, requiring
# `alpha one\nbeta two-typed\n` -- so `:wq` not writing would break it.  Measured on
# a phase 6 tree: it fails, and it fails IDENTICALLY on a phase 5 tree (status 256,
# the file unchanged), because the session opens `f.txt` as a file ARGUMENT and
# phase 5 made that an unknown option.  The editor never reaches the `:wq`.  So the
# failure at 6 is phase 5's, a stage holding 2 and 6 holds 4, and `apart 2 4`
# forbids it already -- exactly as for `apart 2 5` above.
# Phase 5's check states that this phase frees no libc symbol -- `cmp` of the
# stage's starting undefined set against the one it made, "nothing this cut removed
# was libc's last caller".  Phase 6 removes six.  Measured by running the two as one
# stage, `tools/phaserun.sh zero 5-6`: phase 5's check fails with `the libc surface
# moved, and this phase frees nothing` and names chmod, fchmod, fstat, ftruncate,
# lstat and unlink.  Inside a stage every check compares with the STAGE's start, so
# the two cannot share a sweep.  Phase 4's check fails on a phase 6 tree too, but
# only for the reasons `apart 4 5` already records -- it names EDIT_STDIN and four
# others as the argv phase's, and phase 5 took them.
apart       5 6

#        name         phases
package  seed         0
package  build        1
package  terminal     2
package  harness      3
package  streams      4 5
package  files        6

#     phase       needs    kind        why
uses  build:1     seed:0   mechanical  tools/zerodelta.sh refuses without .reference/zero-baselines, which phase 0 records
uses  terminal:2  seed:0   mechanical  the same: its declared delta is checked against those baselines
uses  harness:3   seed:0   mechanical  it is measured against the baselines phase 0 records, in the shape phase 0 now records them
uses  harness:3   terminal:2  rationale   the delta it proves is phase 2's: the warnings it removed are what the new instrument sees
uses  streams:4   seed:0   mechanical  tools/zerodelta.sh compares its six declared records with the baselines phase 0 records
uses  streams:4   terminal:2  mechanical  check_tty() has nothing left to ask only because phase 2 took its other branch, so isatty goes from five calls to four here
uses  streams:5   seed:0   mechanical  tools/zerodelta.sh compares its six declared command lines with the baselines phase 0 records
uses  streams:5   harness:3  mechanical  the six records that move are tools/zargv.py's, and phase 3 is what put an argv record in a zero recording at all
uses  files:6     seed:0   mechanical  tools/zerodelta.sh compares its two screen cases and six command rows with the baselines phase 0 records
uses  files:6     harness:3  mechanical  the old file-based sweep recorded an exit status, and :write went from E32 to E492 without changing it; phase 3's message-level record is what can see this phase at all
